## Proof

**Definition.** A sequence $s_1 s_2 \cdots s_n$ is antipalindromic if reversing it and swapping $A\leftrightarrow B$ recovers the original. Equivalently, for every $i$, the characters at positions $i$ and $n+1-i$ are *opposite* (one is $A$, the other $B$).

**Setup.** Let $n=2020$. Since $n$ is even, the positions split into $n/2 = 1010$ disjoint pairs
$$P_j = \{j,\; n+1-j\}, \qquad j = 1,2,\dots,1010.$$
In any antipalindromic sequence, each pair $P_j$ contains exactly one $A$ and exactly one $B$. Conversely, any choice of one position per pair to hold the $A$ (with the other holding $B$) produces an antipalindromic sequence. Hence the set of antipalindromic sequences is in bijection with the $2^{1010}$ ways to pick, independently for each pair $P_j$, which of its two positions carries an $A$.

**Factoring the sum.** The cost of a sequence is $\prod_{i:\, s_i=A} i$. Since the choice within each pair is independent of every other pair, the cost factors as a product over pairs: for pair $P_j=\{j,n+1-j\}$, the local factor is
- $j$ if position $j$ is the $A$, or
- $n+1-j$ if position $n+1-j$ is the $A$.

Summing the cost over *all* antipalindromic sequences therefore factors as
$$\sum_{\text{antipal.}} \text{cost} \;=\; \prod_{j=1}^{1010}\Big(j + (n+1-j)\Big) \;=\; \prod_{j=1}^{1010}(n+1).$$

Each factor equals $n+1 = 2021$, and there are $1010$ factors, so
$$\sum_{\text{antipal.}} \text{cost} \;=\; 2021^{1010}.$$

**Sanity check on the example.** For $ABBAAB$ (length $6$), the pairs are $\{1,6\},\{2,5\},\{3,4\}$, and the formula gives $(1+6)(2+5)(3+4)=7^3=343$. The $8$ antipalindromic sequences of length $6$ have costs summing to $343$, consistent with the factoring argument. (The given sequence itself has cost $1\cdot4\cdot5=20$, matching the stated example.)

**Answer.** The sum of the costs of all antipalindromic sequences of length $2020$ is

$$\boxed{2021^{1010}}.$$

